description:
You are given two arrays of 32-bit signed integers: a main array and a second array that is checked as a candidate subarray. Implement two functions:

1) A predicate that, given a valid starting index, determines whether the candidate array matches the contiguous segment of the main array starting at that index. Assumptions: the candidate array’s length is less than or equal to the main array’s length; the starting index is within bounds, specifically 0 <= starting_index <= main_length - candidate_length. Return true if the segment of the main array of length equal to the candidate array, beginning at the given starting index, is element-wise equal to the candidate array; otherwise, return false.

2) A predicate that determines whether the candidate array occurs as a contiguous subarray anywhere in the main array. Assumption: the candidate array’s length is less than or equal to the main array’s length. Return true if and only if there exists some starting index k with 0 <= k <= (main_length - candidate_length) such that, for all positions t with 0 <= t < candidate_length, main[k + t] equals candidate[t]. The empty candidate array is permitted and is considered to occur at any index within the main array (including at the end).

All indexing must be within bounds. The returned booleans must exactly reflect the mathematical definitions above: equality for the fixed-index check, and existential occurrence for the anywhere-in-main check.
