description:
Given a finite list of 32-bit integers, return a new list consisting of exactly those elements that occur exactly once in the input, preserving their original relative order. Also provide a helper that, given a list and a target value, returns the number of times the value occurs in the list. Assume the input size can be zero or more. The helper’s result must equal the mathematical count of positions whose element equals the target value. The output list must equal the input list filtered by the predicate “the element’s frequency in the entire input is exactly 1,” with no reordering and no additional elements. During iteration, correctness must be maintained at every step: the loop index remains within the bounds of the input size, the partial result after processing any k elements equals the filter of the first k elements by the uniqueness predicate, and the helper’s counter over any processed prefix equals the precise number of matches in that prefix. The procedures must terminate after processing all elements.
fn count_frequency(arr: &Vec<i32>, key: i32) -> (frequency: usize)
fn remove_duplicates(arr: &Vec<i32>) -> (unique_arr: Vec<i32>)

